## What happened

`DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS because Apple's security frameworks (`Security.framework` / `CryptoKit`) do not implement DSA. On macOS, .NET's crypto layer delegates to the platform — and Apple dropped DSA support entirely. On Linux (.NET uses OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG/CAPI), DSA is still available.

This has been the case since **.NET 7**. If your .NET 10 macOS builds were working, you likely had OpenSSL loaded (e.g., via Homebrew) or were using a managed/third-party implementation without realizing it. The .NET 11 upgrade may have changed how the runtime resolves the crypto provider, surfacing the platform gap.

## How to fix it

You have three options, in order of preference:

### 1. Migrate to a modern algorithm (best long-term)
DSA is considered legacy. If you control the signature format, switch to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported on all platforms.

### 2. Use BouncyCastle for cross-platform DSA verification
If you must verify existing DSA signatures:

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

### 3. Use `DSAOpenSsl` explicitly on macOS
Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and use the OpenSSL-specific class:

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA CreateDsa()
{
    if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
        return new DSAOpenSsl(); // requires OpenSSL dylib on PATH
    return DSA.Create();
}
```

This adds a runtime dependency on `libssl` on macOS, so option **1 or 2** is generally preferable.
